{"type": "thinking", "text": "So:\n- Section ESTRADIOL, item first line \"Oral\"\n- Section GNRHA, item first line \"Injection\"\n- Section CUSTOM, item first line \"Custom\"\n\nThis suggests the category label line is actually the route/form? \"Oral\" for tablet, \"Injection\" for injection, and \"Custom\" for custom category (maybe route defaults to Custom?). Hmm. Could be intended: the category label for medicine list might be the route (\"Oral\", \"Injection\") not category. But then why section headers show categories? Redundant but plausible? Let's check the medicine editor: \"Category\" chips are Estradiol/Antiandrogen/etc. The list uses section headers by category. The first line inside item could be route (Oral/Injection). For custom, route maybe \"Custom\" because no route chosen? But in Add medicine custom there is \"Preparation form\" Tablet/Capsule, so route would be Oral too. Yet it shows \"Custom\".\n\nHmm, maybe the first line is \"category label\" resolved from a resource that for Estradiol category is \"Oral\" and for GnRHa is \"Injection\" — clearly wrong. Why would they resolve to route labels? Because of a mapping bug in shared string resources: category label resource IDs mixed up with route/form labels. This is exactly the change \"category labels are now resolved from shared string resources\". It's plausible that Estradiol category label resource string = \"Oral\" (bug) and GnRHa = \"Injection\".\n\nBut maybe the first line is the \"route\" field computed from preparation form: Tablet → Oral, Injection → Injection, and for custom category the route is category? Let's test by creating an Antiandrogen tablet: route would be Oral (same as Estradiol) or \"Antiandrogen\"? If Antiandrogen item shows \"Oral\", then the first line is route, and Custom showing \"Custom\" would be because custom category doesn't have a mapped route? Hmm, then would be \"Custom\" label. Let's test Antiandrogen quickly. Choose category Antiandrogen, default Spironolactone Tablet 20/25/50/100. Save with 25mg. Then list item line. If \"Oral\", it's route. If \"Antiandrogen\", category label consistent.\n\nAlso SERM default Raloxifene tablet → route Oral. Let's test Antiandrogen. But note user data grows. Fine, later archive all.\n\nTap Add medicine."}
