{"type": "thinking", "text": "Good.\n\nNow, should I report the unit-switch non-conversion? Let me consider the bug's reproducibility and expected behavior. We can reproduce:\n1. Edit a result, enter 150 in pg/mL.\n2. Tap pmol/L chip.\n3. Value stays 150; target becomes 367–734 pmol/L; badge changes to Below.\nThis is arguably a bug: changing the unit changes the target range but not the entered value, so the same test result abruptly becomes out-of-range. Expected: value should convert (150 pg/mL → 550.6 pmol/L) or be cleared so user re-enters. But there is no spec. In many lab apps, units chips switch display units and convert. Given the app converted when the preferred unit changed in settings, it demonstrates the app has conversion logic; not applying it in the editor is inconsistent.\n\nHowever, note the UI design: The unit chips are buttons for \"unit of the value I'm typing\". The user might switch units before typing. If the field already has a value, keeping the number is equivalent to reinterpreting it, which is dangerous. But maybe the app treats the unit selection as part of the result value and does not auto-convert by design.\n\nCould report as \"wrong_value\" with a clear expected: \"Switching unit should convert the entered value (or clear it) so the result isn't silently reinterpreted.\" Observed: value 150 remains while unit changes pg/mL→pmol/L and target range changes, turning an in-range result into below-range.\n\nThis is within \"lab test results under Calibration\" and units changed area. If confirmed by developer, good. But if developer says by design, no harm? The task says confirmed reports are logged; a report rejected is not necessarily harmful, but we want real bugs. Let's keep this as a candidate.\n\nBut first let's look for stronger bugs.\n\nLet's examine the \"unit preference\" behavior more: When we changed settings preferred unit, the result card's value converted to 550.6. Then editing showed 150 pg/mL (original unit). The selected unit chip pg/mL was checked, not the preferred pmol/L. So the edit screen ignores the global preferred unit. But is that \"wrong\"? The record unit is pg/mL. Hmm.\n\nMaybe there's a clearer bug: After changing preferred unit to pmol/L, summary card shows 550.6 pmol/L, but the result edit still shows 150 pg/mL. Suppose the user now edits the result but doesn't change anything and saves. What happens? The stored value stays canonical (150 pg/mL). Since edit shows 150 pg/mL, no change. Fine.\n\nBut if the user switches to pmol/L and saves (without retyping), the canonical value changes to 150 pmol/L = 40.9 pg/mL. That's the unit-switch bug.\n\nAnother potential bug: When switching units in the result editor, the \"Above/Below/In range\" status is recalculated based on the unchanged number, not converted, which creates a false \"Below\". This is a visible wrong state.\n\nI think it's worth reporting. But let's see if there's an even clearer issue related to units fetched with test list. Maybe units for a test can be missing when added later due to asynchronous loading. We saw them all. Hmm.\n\nCould there be a bug with the custom analyte SHBG and preferred unit? We set SHBG's only unit nmol/L, so no switch.\n\nLet's test adding a result immediately after selecting a test, where units fetched with the list; maybe the unit chips are briefly missing, but no.\n\nLet's test a test with a single unit—SHBG result saving and display. We didn't save SHBG. Could do: add a new test result with only SHBG, save, verify summary card displays SHBG value and unit. This tests custom analyte path and units. But not necessary for changed area? It is related.\n\nLet's test saving a result with FSH only vs all tests. Not needed.\n\nLet's focus on group generation deeper. Potential bug: When setting start date in the past, the group's START TIME automatically becomes 12:00 AM. Generated doses at 3:00 PM each day. But what if the group is created after a specific time of day and start date is today? Not relevant.\n\nPotential bug: Generated past doses appear as records, but their medication count may be lost. The record sheet doesn't show count for live either. However, the Plan card shows \"Custom · 5 μg\" but not count; maybe count is not needed.\n\nPotential bug: In the group card in Plan, generated past dates show \"MISSED\" for deleted records; okay.\n\nPotential bug: When you change the group's time of day after records exist, editing is locked (\"some settings can't be changed\"). Fine.\n\nPotential bug: The group editor history generation \"generated past doses are now recorded the same way as doses logged live\" — we should compare a live dose's record and generated one: both show \"Edit record\" with same fields, Delete, Save. We saw that. But what about the group association? Live dose shows \"Scheduled for Nov 14 3:00 PM -12m\"; generated shows \"Scheduled for Nov 13 3:00 PM\". Same.\n\nWhat about the medication's \"dose count\" (e.g., 1 tablet)? Both don't show. Fine.\n\nPotential bug: The generated record for a past dose does not include the \"Scheduled for\" time offset? It does.\n\nPotential bug: Generated doses on start date maybe too many if start time is 12:00 AM vs 3pm. Fine.\n\nPotential bug: The history \"6 RECORDS LOGGED\" count includes deleted? It decreased to 5 after delete. Good.\n\nPotential bug: The group's completed count on Plan for Nov 13 showed 2/3 after deleting one generated record, and the deleted slot became MISSED. But the history monthly calendar might still show the day with 2 records; fine.\n\nPotential bug: When creating a group with \"Mark as completed\" and start date in the past, the generated records are created with the *current* time instead of the scheduled time? We saw 3:00 PM matching the schedule. Good.\n\nPotential bug: Generated records use the group's \"start time\" rather than the daily times. We saw 3:00 PM and 9:00 PM. Good.\n\nPotential bug: Multiple times per day generated two records. Good.\n\nPotential bug: If you select \"Mark as missed\", the group creates no records but Plan shows MISSED. That's expected semantics.\n\nPotential bug: If you select \"Hide\", past doses are hidden; Plan doesn't show them. Good.\n\nThus group generation seems solid.\n\nNow, medicine categories. Let's test creating a SERM medicine (we only viewed the form) and check the list section header and item. We have Estradiol, GnRHa, Custom; Antiandrogen archived. SERM not saved. But category label resolution likely fine. Could still test quickly because changed area specifically. Also test \"Antiandrogen\" archived doesn't appear. Perhaps a bug: archived medicine's category section header persists with no items? It was removed. Good.\n\nMaybe a bug with category labels after archiving all meds of a category: section header gone. Fine.\n\nCould test editing a medicine to change display name and see it reflects in group card. Let's do that with Estradiol. Not changed area but state. We already tested group rename. Medicine name edit could reveal group card not updating. Let's test:\n- Medicines → Estradiol valerate → Edit medicine → change name to \"Estradiol valerate QA\" → save.\n- Check list and Plan group \"QA Missed Group\" chip uses updated name.\n- Then revert name to \"Estradiol valerate\".\nThis is a bit of work but good consistency test. However, our data set is messy; we can do.\n\nLet's go to Medicines."}
