{"type": "thinking", "text": "Good.\n\nNow let's search for a bug in the \"group editor history generation\" using the \"PAST SCHEDULE\" preview or records. Maybe there's a known bug: generated records have the wrong date when the group start date is in the past and start time 12:00 AM? We saw correct dates.\n\nMaybe the generated past doses are not recorded for the *start date* if the start time is after the scheduled time. In our first group start Nov 11 12:00 AM, scheduled 3pm, record created Nov 11. Good. If start time were 4pm and scheduled 3pm, the first dose should start Nov 12, not Nov 11. We didn't test. That's a nuanced behavior: group start date/time semantics. The \"START TIME 12:00 AM\" is the time from which the schedule begins; if scheduled dose is before start time on the start date, should it generate? Likely not. The app sets start time to 12:00 AM when past date selected. If user sets start date today and start time now (3:01 PM), and times of day 3:00 PM, then the 3pm dose is 1 minute before start. Would the app generate a past dose for today at 3pm? If it does, maybe bug. But not in changed area.\n\nMaybe the generated records are marked as \"completed\" but their time is the scheduled time; yes.\n\nMaybe the generated records are recorded with the current date instead of scheduled date when start time is \"Now\"? We didn't use \"Now\" with past date because selecting a past date sets time 12am. We could test a group with start date today, start time 12:00 AM, scheduled time 9:00 AM (past relative to now 3pm), with PAST SCHEDULE? When start date is today and time 12am, does the PAST SCHEDULE section appear? Let's test: create a new group with today's date, set start time to 12:00 AM (instead of Now), add a time of day 9:00 AM, and see if PAST SCHEDULE appears and generates a record for today. If yes, it should create a completed record today at 9am. If no, maybe bug. But this is extra.\n\nGiven the hint \"generated past doses are now recorded the same way as doses logged live\", the planted bug might be that generated doses are missing from history or not counted. We verified they are included. But maybe a more subtle issue: generated doses are recorded with the *current* medication names or strengths? Hmm.\n\nLet's inspect the data consistency between Plan day counts and History counts:\n- Nov 11: Plan 1/1, history 1\n- Nov 12: Plan 1/1, history 1\n- Nov 13: Plan earlier 3/3, history 3; after delete 2/3, history 2\n- Nov 14: Plan 2/3 (logged 2 of 3 scheduled; one QA Multi 3pm unlogged? Actually Plan showed 1/3 earlier; after new groups now 2/4) etc. The counts match logged records. Good.\n\nCould there be a bug with history record count badge on the month calendar vs total? It shows \"5 RECORDS LOGGED\", calendar days show counts. We saw 1,2. Fine.\n\nMaybe a bug with the generated record's time in the \"Scheduled for\" label: For the 9pm generated record it showed \"Scheduled for Nov 13 9:00 PM\" matching. Good.\n\nMaybe a bug with the generated record's date in the edit form: EditText \"Nov 13\" and \"9:00 PM\". Good.\n\nOne thing: When we deleted the generated 9pm record, Plan showed it as MISSED. That matches expected: a past scheduled dose with no record is missed. Good.\n\nCould test \"Mark as completed\" generated records and then edit the group's time of day: locked. Fine.\n\nAt this point, I suspect the intended bug may be in the medicine category picker. Let's examine more: The medicine editor category picker chips are \"Estradiol\", \"Antiandrogen\", \"SERM\", \"GnRHa\", \"Custom\". The list section headers are \"ESTRADIOL\", \"ANTIANDROGEN\", \"GNRHA\", \"CUSTOM\". All correct. But maybe the category label for the medicine details is wrong: it shows \"Oral\" not \"Estradiol\". Could that be the planted bug? Consider this: The medicine details header is supposed to show the category label (\"Estradiol\") but instead shows route (\"Oral\"). The list item also shows \"Oral\". If the category label was resolved from shared string resources in this build, maybe a wrong resource id maps category to the *route* resource (\"Oral\"/\"Injection\"). The fact that section header ESTRADIOL differs from the item's \"Oral\" is a visible inconsistency. A user might see two different category labels for the same medicine. Is that a defect? The task says \"category labels are now resolved from shared string resources.\" If the item label is intended to be the category, then \"Oral\" is wrong. But for GnRHa, \"Injection\" could be both route and maybe category? The category is \"GnRHa\"; route is \"Injection\". For Estradiol, \"Oral\"; route. For Antiandrogen, \"Oral\"; route. So the item's first line is consistently route, not category. That looks intentional.\n\nBut why is the Custom tablet route \"Custom\" rather than \"Oral\"? Because for Custom category, there is no preset route mapping, so it defaults to the category label \"Custom\". This suggests the first line is indeed the route, and custom has no route. Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe the first line is actually the category label *display string*, and for Estradiol category the resource is \"Oral\" (wrong), for Antiandrogen \"Oral\" (wrong), for GnRHa \"Injection\" (wrong), for Custom \"Custom\" (correct). That would be exactly the kind of bug introduced by \"resolved from shared string resources\": the resource lookup may be using the *route* strings for built-in categories instead of category names. But then the section headers use the correct category name strings, and the chip labels use correct category names. Why would the item line use route strings? Maybe because the item line is intentionally the route.\n\nLet's examine the item structure in the medicine list more carefully with screenshot. It shows a colored pill icon, first line \"Oral\" (small grey maybe category), second line \"Estradiol valerate\" (bigger), third \"Tablet · 2 mg\". The first line being \"Oral\" is likely the route (application), while the second line is medicine name. But then the section header \"ESTRADIOL\" is the category. So the list item does not show category, only route. That's a design choice. In the medicine details, first line \"Oral\" + name + form. Again route. The group card chip shows \"Estradiol valerate · Sublingual · 2 mg\" (name · route · strength). So route is a field. Fine.\n\nSo no bug.\n\nMaybe the category picker labels are not all correct in terms of localization? There's no locale switch. Could set system language? Not through device tools directly (no settings). Not needed.\n\nLet's revisit the unit-switch issue. It might be the intended planted bug related to \"units for each test are now fetched together with the test list\". Another possible bug: The units are fetched with the test list but the **target range conversion** may use the wrong factor for custom units or when editing. We tested all 3 units for Estradiol: pg/mL target 100–200, ng/dL target 10–20, pmol/L target 367–734. Correct.\n\nTest Testosterone units: ng/dL target <50; switch to ng/mL (<5?), nmol/L (<1.735?). We can check if target conversion is correct. Let's test quickly in the edit form: tap Testosterone ng/mL and see target. Expected: 50 ng/dL = 0.5 ng/mL? Wait 1 ng/mL = 100 ng/dL, so 50 ng/dL = 0.5 ng/mL. 50 ng/dL = 1.735 nmol/L. If the app shows wrong conversion, that's a clear bug. Let's test.\n\nOpen result edit. Currently on Calibration with 150. Tap card, then tap Testosterone unit chips. We'll do it."}
